sition Mode Selector**, which chooses:\n\n- Echo only (various single/multi‑head combinations)\n- Echo + spring reverb\n- Reverb only\n\nTypical breakdown (as documented in various manuals and modern RE‑201 emulations):\n\n- Modes 1–4: **echo only** using different single or combined playback heads  \n- Modes 5–11: **echo + reverb** (same head combinations as 1–4 plus variants with more heads)  \n- Mode 12: **reverb only** (spring tank, no echo)\n\nA common way this is described (and mirrored in the RE‑20 / software):\n\n- One head = single delay\n- Two heads = **multi‑tap rhythmic patterns**\n- Three heads = **dense repeating echo**; with spring reverb engaged, this is the classic “maxed‑out Space Echo” dub sound.\n\n**URls:**\n\n- Roland official RE‑201 (software) description:  \n  `https://www.roland.com/global/products/rc_re-201_space_echo/`  \n- Roland story / overview:  \n  `https://rolandcorp.com.au/blog/the-roland-re-201-space-echo-story`  \n\n---\n\n### b) Delay time range\n\nDifferent documents quote slightly different numbers; the hardware is continuously variable via **Repeat Rate** (tape speed). A typical set of figures from a RE‑201‑style manual:\n\n- **Head 1:** ~69–177 ms  \n- **Head 2:** ~131–337 ms  \n- **Head 3:** ~189–489 ms  \n\nBecause the heads are equally spaced, head 2 ≈ 2× head 1, head 3 ≈ 3× head 1 (for a given tape speed). Many vintage‑spec sources summarize this as **short–medium delays up to roughly 300–500 ms** on the original hardware.\n\n---\n\n### c) Spring reverb\n\n- **Type:** real **3‑spring mechanical tank** inside the unit  \n- **Topology:** parallel to the echo path; can be used:\n  - Alone (Mode 12)\n  - In combination with any head selection (modes 5–11) via the Mode Selector\n- Front panel usually has **separate Reverb Level** control, independent of Echo Level / Repeat Rate.\n\nThe spring contributes the characteristic “boingy” ambience that blends with the smeared tape repeats in classic dub.\n\n**URLs:**\n\n- General tech overview:  \n  `https://www.effectsdatabase.com/model/roland/re201`  \n- Vintage review describing 3‑spring tank:  \n  `https://musictech.com/reviews/roland-re201-space-echo/`  \n\n---\n\n## 2. Roland Dimension D SDD‑320\n\n### a) Architecture and BBD chorus\n\nThe Dimension D is **not a modulation monster** in the flanger sense; it is a relatively subtle **BBD‑based chorus / spatial enhancer**.\n\n- **Delay line type:** **Bucket‑brigade device (BBD)**\n- **Topology:** Two short BBD delay lines, slightly offset and modulated with very low‑depth, low‑rate LFOs.\n- **Goal:** Gentle, *time‑varying* decorrelation between left / right channels without obvious pitch warble.\n\nThe LFO depth and rates are deliberately small, so you get **thickness and “dimension”** with almost no obvious chorus movement.\n\n---\n\n### b) What the four buttons do\n\nThe original SDD‑320 front panel has four rectangular pushbuttons labelled **1, 2, 3, 4**. You can press multiple at once; they are *not* mutually exclusive.\n\nRoland’s documentation (and later remakes) describe them roughly as:\n\n- **Mode 1:**  \n  - Mild widening and thickening; the “always on” studio mode.  \n  - Lowest modulation depth.\n- **Mode 2:**  \n  - Slightly deeper chorus; still subtle, typical for guitars / synths.\n- **Mode 3:**  \n  - Stronger, more obvious chorus; often used for pads and keys.\n- **Mode 4:**  \n  - The most pronounced; more obvious movement and width.  \n\nCombining modes (e.g. **1+2**, **1+3**) engages **different internal delay / LFO combinations**, giving intermediate flavors.\n\nA recent Roland manual for SDD‑320 emulation lists these as **“Dimension Modes 1–4”**, each selecting a preset combination of delay time, LFO rate and depth.\n\n**URL (manual scans / references):**\n\n- SDD‑320 owner’s manual (archived):  \n  `https://ia800302.us.archive.org/31/items/SDD-320_owners_manual/SDD-320_owners_manual_text.pdf`  \n\n---\n\n### c) Why it stays mono‑compatible\n\nThe Dimension D was designed for **broadcast and studio use** when mono compatibility was critical:\n\n- Left and right channels use **matched, low‑depth modulated delays**.\n- The modulation is:\n  - Very **slow and shallow**  \n  - Often designed so that **sum (L+R)** collapses cleanly, without comb‑filtering that would dramatically change the tone.\n- The design minimizes:\n  - Large static phase offsets  \n  - Large delay differences between channels at any instant\n\nResult: When summed to mono, the decorrelated early reflections and chorus largely **cancel to a “centered” tone** rather than creating big notches or phasing, which is why engineers love it on vocals and busses.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Juno‑60 / Juno‑106 Chorus\n\nBoth Junos use **classic BBD chorus** with fixed modes, but there are differences in implementation and LFO rates.\n\n### a) General architecture\n\n- **Type:** BBD‑based stereo chorus\n- **Topology:**  \n  - Mono input → BBD lines → pseudo‑stereo output  \n  - LFO modulates the BBD clock → pitch‑modulated delay\n- **Noise:** Classic **chorus hiss**, part of the signature.\n\n---\n\n### b) Modes and LFO behavior\n\nBoth Juno‑60 and Juno‑106 have **two dedicated chorus buttons** and allow **both on**:\n\n- **Mode I (Button 1):**\n  - Slower LFO\n  - Shallower modulation\n  - Subtle widening, gentle “swim”\n- **Mode II (Button 2):**\n  - Faster LFO\n  - Deeper modulation\n  - More pronounced chorus, noticeable wobble\n- **Mode I+II (both buttons):**\n  - Internally combines two LFOs / delay settings\n  - Creates richer, almost ensemble‑like modulation\n\nApproximate LFO rates (varies by measurement, but broadly consistent with analyses and service docs):\n\n- **Juno‑60:**\n  - Mode I ≈ 0.3–0.4 Hz  \n  - Mode II ≈ 0.6–0.8 Hz  \n- **Juno‑106:**\n  - Similar ranges, but with minor component changes that make many users describe 106 as slightly **darker** and somewhat **smoother**.\n\n---\n\n### c) BBD details\n\nWhile different tech notes give slightly different device IDs, the general structure is:\n\n- **Dual BBD lines** (one per channel) using MN3xxx‑series or similar\n- A shared LFO or phase‑related LFOs controlling BBD clock\n- Fixed delay times and depth per mode, *not* continuously variable like a standalone chorus pedal\n\nThis fixed‑mode, dual‑line BBD design is part of why the Juno chorus is so recognizable: you essentially always hear *one of three* stereotyped chorusing textures.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Roland Space Echo: Mode Selector Details\n\nTo make this explicit: the **Mode Selector** on the RE‑201 is a **12‑position rotary switch** choosing which playback heads and/or spring reverb are active.\n\nA typical interpretation aligned with Roland documentation and RE‑20 manual:\n\n- **Heads:** 1, 2, 3  \n  - Delay times proportional to tape travel:  \n    - Head 1 = base delay  \n    - Head 2 ≈ 2× Head 1  \n    - Head 3 ≈ 3× Head 1\n- **Modes 1–4:**  \n  - Various combinations:  \n    - One head (1, 2, or 3)  \n    - Two heads together (e.g. 2+3) for multi‑tap echoes\n- **Modes 5–11:**  \n  - Identical head combinations as 1–4, but with **spring reverb engaged**.\n- **Mode 12:**  \n  - **Reverb only**, no echo\n\nFor dub / techno:\n\n- Classic trick: choose **multi‑head mode (e.g. all three)**, set **Repeat Rate relatively slow**, crank **Intensity** close to self‑oscillation, and ride **Echo Level** for feedback swells.\n- Switching between modes mid‑note changes the rhythmic pattern of the repeats because you’re changing which heads feed back.\n\nA convenient modern manual that explains this behaviour (for the RE‑20, but explicitly referencing the RE‑201 topology):\n\n- `https://media.sweetwater.com/store/media/re-20_om.pdf`  \n\n---\n\n## 5. Making the Low End Mono: Why, Where, and Frequencies\n\n### a) Why engineers mono the low end\n\nEngineers often **sum low frequencies to mono** (or at least constrain stereo width) for three main reasons:\n\n1. **Vinyl cutting limitations**  \n   - On vinyl, **lateral (side‑to‑side)** motion carries the **mid (mono)** content; **vertical** motion carries the **side (difference)** content.  \n   - Strong, out‑of‑phase bass in the side channel causes large vertical excursions, which can:\n     - Make the stylus **jump** or mistrack\n     - Force the cutting engineer to **narrow the stereo** or roll off bass  \n   - To avoid this, mastering engineers historically:\n     - Used **elliptical equalizers** or **sum‑and‑difference filters** to **mono the low end**.\n\n2. **Translation and focus**  \n   - Even in digital / streaming contexts, mono bass:\n     - **Centers the groove**; kick and bass feel locked and solid.\n     - Avoids low‑frequency phase cancellation when playback is mono or on club systems with mono subs.\n\n3. **Club systems and PAs**  \n   - Many club / live systems run **subs in mono**, with an active crossover feeding mono low‑frequency drivers.  \n   - If your mix has wide, phasey low end, it may **collapse unpredictably** on those systems; mono‑izing bass avoids this.\n\nAn example mastering article explicitly advising this:\n\n- Quantara (vinyl mastering guide):  \n  `https://www.quantaramusic.com/learn/how-to-master-for-vinyl`  \n\nThey suggest summing low frequencies toward mono below **~100–150 Hz** and checking the track in mono for bass phase issues.\n\n---\n\n### b) Typical crossover frequencies\n\nThere is **no single “standard” frequency**, but practice clusters around:\n\n- **100 Hz** – very common, especially in conservative vinyl cuts or very bass‑heavy material.\n- **120 Hz** – often cited as a balance point where most fundamental bass content is still captured, but higher‑frequency stereo info remains.\n- **150 Hz** – sometimes used for very loud or challenging cuts, or as an upper bound in recommendations like “mono below 100–150 Hz”.\n\nModern mastering approaches:\n\n- Many engineers use **linear phase or minimum‑phase elliptical filters** or **mid/side EQ**:\n  - Example practice:  \n    - Fully mono below **80–100 Hz**  \n    - Gradually reducing width between **100–200 Hz** rather than a hard brick wall\n- The exact frequency depends on:\n  - Musical genre (e.g. dub techno vs acoustic)\n  - Vinyl side length and level (hotter, shorter cuts need more care)\n  - The cutting engineer’s own lathe, cutter head, and house EQ.\n\nAgain, the Quantara vinyl guide puts it succinctly:  \n> “sum low-frequency energy toward mono below roughly 100–150 Hz” and check in mono for low‑end phase issues.  \n`https://www.quantaramusic.com/learn/how-to-master-for-vinyl`\n\nOther mastering engineers and Sound On Sound articles generally echo the **80–150 Hz** guideline.\n\n---\n\n### c) Direct link between vinyl practice and modern “mono low end”\n\nHistorically:\n\n- Vinyl cutting rooms used **elliptical equalizers** (e.g. Neumann, Ortofon) that:\n  - Converted audio to **M/S**\n  - Applied a **frequency‑dependent high‑pass to the side channel**, effectively making bass mono.\n\nModern DAW equivalents:\n\n- Dedicated plugins (“elliptical EQ”, “bass mono”), or:\n  - M/S EQ with **side HPF at 80–120 Hz**\n  - Mid/Side wideners that restrict width below a selected frequency\n\nAlthough we mostly listen on digital formats now, the **habit and sonic benefits** stayed:\n\n- **Solid, punchy center** for kick & bass\n- **Consistent translation** on club PAs and small speakers\n- Less risk if a track is later cut to vinyl\n\n---\n\nIf you want, I can next:\n\n- Lay out **exact head‑combination diagrams** for the RE‑201 modes (1–12), or  \n- Suggest specific **Bass‑to‑mono chains** (e.g. mid/side routing and filter slopes) tailored to dub/techno master bus setups.","refusal":null,"reasoning":null,"annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.roland.com/global/products/rc_re-201_space_echo/","title":"RE-201 Space Echo | Software Effect","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://help.uaudio.com/hc/en-us/articles/33538271334164-Roland-RE-201-Space-Echo-Manual","title":"Roland RE-201 Space Echo Manual","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.notice-facile.com/en/manual/1400721/roland+space-echo-re-201","title":"Space Echo RE-201 - User manual and instructions - Notice Facile","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.roland.com/us/products/rc_re-201_space_echo/","title":"RE-201 Space Echo | Software Effect - Roland","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.effectsdatabase.com/model/roland/re201","title":"Roland RE-201 Space Echo | Effects Database","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.musicradar.com/news/producers-guide-roland-space-echo","title":"The producer's guide to the Roland RE-201 Space Echo","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://rolandcorp.com.au/blog/the-roland-re-201-space-echo-story","title":"The Roland RE-201 Space Echo Story","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.quantaramusic.com/learn/how-to-master-for-vinyl","title":"How to Master for Vinyl — Pre-Master Guide | Quantara","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://musictech.com/reviews/roland-re201-space-echo/","title":"Vintage: Roland RE-201 Space Echo","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://articles.roland.com/tips-and-tricks-roland-cloud-re-201-space-echo/","title":"Tips and Tricks: Roland Cloud RE-201 Space Echo","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://static.roland.com/manuals/jx-08_reference/eng/17811918.html","title":"SDD-320","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://media.sweetwater.com/store/media/re-20_om.pdf","title":"[PDF] re-20_om.pdf - Sweetwater","sta